WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.677
-This is great. I mean,

00:00:01.677 --> 00:00:02.869
you've been on the show

00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:03.870
since the pandemic started,

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.605
but you were just
a disembodied head

00:00:05.605 --> 00:00:08.908
and it's so great
to be reminded that you

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:10.910
have a whole human body.
-Allegedly.

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:12.645
-Allegedly.
-But yes.

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:14.147
Yes. I know.
Last time we talked,

00:00:14.147 --> 00:00:16.015
I was crashing
on my friend's couch in L.A.

00:00:16.015 --> 00:00:17.316
We were Zooming.

00:00:17.316 --> 00:00:19.419
I was in like a velvet onesie

00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:22.022
and, now, I can't breathe
in a leather skirt.

00:00:22.022 --> 00:00:24.490
-Do you do you enjoy
having a reason

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:25.525
to get dressed up again?

00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:26.659
-Oh, yeah.
-Oh, good.

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:28.495
-I mean, I don't know about you,
but I started --

00:00:28.495 --> 00:00:29.562
a couple months ago,
I started like,

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:31.030
even if I'd go
to the grocery store,

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:32.432
I'd throw on mascara,

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:34.299
-No, same thing.

00:00:34.299 --> 00:00:35.768
-Same with you, yeah.

00:00:35.768 --> 00:00:37.002
Which, in the moment,
feels good

00:00:37.002 --> 00:00:38.004
and then, you get home
and you're like --

00:00:38.004 --> 00:00:39.038
[ Inhales sharply ]

00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:41.106
Ah, it's kind of missing
the little...

00:00:41.106 --> 00:00:42.842
-Right.
-oomph of like going out,

00:00:42.842 --> 00:00:44.343
but, yeah.
-Sure.

00:00:44.343 --> 00:00:45.778
You were in L.A.,
as you mentioned.

00:00:45.778 --> 00:00:46.879
You're back in the city,

00:00:46.879 --> 00:00:48.448
You've been back in the city
this year.

00:00:48.448 --> 00:00:49.716
How do you feel?

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:53.652
Because I am feeling
very bullish about New York.

00:00:53.652 --> 00:00:55.155
-Bullish?
-Yeah, like I'm feeling

00:00:55.155 --> 00:00:56.689
positive about it, you know...
-Yes.

00:00:56.689 --> 00:00:57.924
-...upbeat, optimistic.

00:00:57.924 --> 00:00:59.225
-I thought you were like,
"I'm feeling like I want

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:00.926
to run at it and gore it."

00:01:00.926 --> 00:01:02.695
[ Laughter ]
-I want to take it apart.

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:04.663
-[ Laughing ] Take it apart.

00:01:04.663 --> 00:01:05.898
I'm feeling very hopeful.

00:01:05.898 --> 00:01:07.533
I rode the subway
for the first time.

00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:08.667
I wept.

00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:09.702
-You did?!
-I did.

00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:11.337
-On the subway?
-Yeah.

00:01:11.337 --> 00:01:15.241
Because I couldn't believe
that it's been over a year

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:18.978
and I couldn't believe
that like we're all --

00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:21.748
we're slowly getting back to,
I don't know,

00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:24.350
like the New York
that I fell in love with,

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:25.518
you know, when I moved here,

00:01:25.518 --> 00:01:27.387
and even the New York
that I grew up watching.

00:01:27.387 --> 00:01:29.055
And, obviously,
it's different, but.

00:01:29.055 --> 00:01:30.556
Like the other day,
I went with a friend.

00:01:30.556 --> 00:01:32.391
We rode the Staten Island Ferry

00:01:32.391 --> 00:01:33.626
-Look at you.
-just to ride it.

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:35.027
[ Laughs ]

00:01:35.027 --> 00:01:37.029
-My takeaway here is that

00:01:37.029 --> 00:01:38.898
you were away
for just long enough

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:41.467
that you've come back a tourist
who is here for the first time.

00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:42.835
-That's pretty much
what happened

00:01:42.835 --> 00:01:45.305
because I got here in December
and I was like, "What?"

00:01:45.305 --> 00:01:46.739
Because it was just in time

00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:48.942
for like two blizzards
and no one to leave

00:01:48.942 --> 00:01:50.677
and it was a nightmare here.

00:01:50.677 --> 00:01:52.045
And we were riding
the Staten Island Ferry

00:01:52.045 --> 00:01:53.479
the other day and then,
when we got off,

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:55.347
like in the downtown --

00:01:55.347 --> 00:01:58.851
I don't know where that is,
but like where the thing goes --

00:01:58.851 --> 00:02:00.285
a woman almost ran me over
with her bike

00:02:00.285 --> 00:02:01.921
and she was like, "Move!"

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:04.523
And I was like,
"We're coming back!

00:02:04.523 --> 00:02:06.358
Like look at that!"

00:02:06.358 --> 00:02:09.262
-Wait, so had you ever ridden
the Staten Island Ferry before?

00:02:09.262 --> 00:02:10.263
-Oh, yeah.
-You had?

00:02:10.263 --> 00:02:11.297
-I like it.
-Gotcha.

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:12.331
-Because you see everything.

00:02:12.331 --> 00:02:13.766
-No, I mean, I would imagine.

00:02:13.766 --> 00:02:16.002
I, obviously, am revealing
here that I have not.

00:02:16.002 --> 00:02:18.171
-Oh, it's a ball.
I mean, is it a ball?

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:19.539
It's a ball.
-Gotcha.

00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:22.007
And so do you get off
in Staten Island --

00:02:22.007 --> 00:02:23.009
-Yeah, and then,
you just like...

00:02:23.009 --> 00:02:24.010
♪ Doot doot doot doot ♪

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.377
...you like do, do, do-do-loo

00:02:25.377 --> 00:02:27.046
through the lobby
and then, you get back on,

00:02:27.046 --> 00:02:28.848
you ride it again. [ Laughs ]
-Wow!

00:02:28.848 --> 00:02:30.650
-it's great like
if you want to be --

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:31.851
like those moments
when you feel like

00:02:31.851 --> 00:02:34.287
you're like a tourist
in your own hometown.

00:02:34.287 --> 00:02:35.488
Like my friend and I rode it.

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:36.789
We ate snacks.

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:39.592
We like sat on the back
and looked at the skyline.

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:40.893
That was the rail.

00:02:40.893 --> 00:02:42.929
And we were like,
"We live here.

00:02:42.929 --> 00:02:44.596
This is so majestic."
Because, right now,

00:02:44.596 --> 00:02:45.965
we're all like inside.

00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:47.567
We're scared.
We're on our phones.

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:49.068
-That is so lovely.
-It was really lovely.

00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:50.069
And it's open air.

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:52.238
-I don't mean to go back
too far,

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:55.708
but did anyone on the subway
notice you were crying about it?

00:02:55.708 --> 00:02:57.610
-[ Laughs ] No, I hope not.
-Yeah.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.411
-The crying I was doing,
it was gentle.

00:02:59.411 --> 00:03:00.612
It was like very like
"The Crown."

00:03:00.612 --> 00:03:02.081
I was like gazing out...
-Oh.

00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:03.349
-...like just like it wouldn't

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:05.651
break the water line.
-I think every character

00:03:05.651 --> 00:03:07.219
on "The Crown" would also cry,

00:03:07.219 --> 00:03:08.520
if they were
on the New York City subway.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:09.755
[ Laughter ]
-Exactly.

00:03:09.755 --> 00:03:13.559
Like stoically,
as like orchestra like swelled

00:03:13.559 --> 00:03:15.795
and there was like a slow push.
-Something went terribly wrong

00:03:15.795 --> 00:03:18.364
when the queen visited New York
and ended up on the subway.

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:21.133
-And she ended up
on the subway, yeah.

00:03:21.133 --> 00:03:23.068
[ Laughs ]
-You have a wonderful new show

00:03:23.068 --> 00:03:25.404
-Thank you.
-and I want to talk about --

00:03:25.404 --> 00:03:26.773
This is the first scene

00:03:26.773 --> 00:03:28.541
you filmed.
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:30.810
-So, you basically like burst

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:32.178
-Forth.
-out of the ground.

00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:33.979
-I burst out from the desert,
from the ground,

00:03:33.979 --> 00:03:35.815
and it looks like
it's like a --

00:03:35.815 --> 00:03:38.117
It's a beautiful shot,
but I'm like blasted

00:03:38.117 --> 00:03:40.552
from the earth
through a water main.

00:03:40.552 --> 00:03:45.458
-Now, this was not like --

00:03:45.458 --> 00:03:46.959
Excuse me.
This was not special effects

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:49.095
that mean you did not have
to be submerged in water?

00:03:49.095 --> 00:03:52.365
-No. They drove us
to the desert,

00:03:52.365 --> 00:03:53.499
where they had buried
-You're doing a lot

00:03:53.499 --> 00:03:54.767
of desert work recently.
-I know, there's

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:56.235
a lot desert work.
I know.

00:03:56.235 --> 00:03:58.070
-I mean, "Palm Springs"
was fantastic.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:03:59.505
-Thank you.
-Such good desert work from you.

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:00.573
-[ Laughs ]
-Obviously, they saw it
and they're like,

00:04:00.573 --> 00:04:03.409
"She's our preeminent
desert actress."

00:04:03.409 --> 00:04:04.944
-Yeah. They were like,
"Let's see

00:04:04.944 --> 00:04:06.612
what else we can do
to her out there."

00:04:06.612 --> 00:04:08.414
By the way, is that Andy

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:10.083
and does it say,
"My doink fell off!"?

00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:13.319
-Yes. This is your costar
Andy Samberg,

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:15.221
saying, "My doink fell off!"
-His doink fell off.

00:04:15.221 --> 00:04:16.455
Okay.

00:04:16.455 --> 00:04:18.524
He played a very popular

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:21.227
oc-curring character...

00:04:21.227 --> 00:04:22.361
[ Laughter ]

00:04:22.361 --> 00:04:23.729
...who was a mummy.

00:04:23.729 --> 00:04:25.431
And this is now available

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:27.700
-Okay.
-in the NBC Experience Store,

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:30.202
which is not currently open.

00:04:30.202 --> 00:04:31.637
-Can I see the picture?

00:04:31.637 --> 00:04:32.638
-Of his face?

00:04:32.638 --> 00:04:33.706
-Yeah, his eyes are closed.

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:34.907
-Yeah, his eyes are closed.

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:36.275
-[ Laughing ]
That's so funny.

00:04:36.275 --> 00:04:38.077
-He played a mummy on the show,
whose doink fell off.

00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:39.112
-Wow.
-Would you believe,

00:04:39.112 --> 00:04:40.379
having spent time with him,

00:04:40.379 --> 00:04:42.382
that he wrote the line,
"My doink fell off!"

00:04:42.382 --> 00:04:43.749
-God, would I believe that.

00:04:43.749 --> 00:04:45.551
[ Laughter ]
Andy Samberg?

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:47.053
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah,
the Andy Samberg.

00:04:47.053 --> 00:04:49.288
-This guy?
-Our preeminent doink actor.

00:04:49.288 --> 00:04:51.591
[ Laughter ]
This is how you had to sit.

00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:53.792
-Yes. So they drove me
to the desert,

00:04:53.792 --> 00:04:56.629
where they'd buried a tank
of water in the ground.

00:04:56.629 --> 00:04:59.231
-Yeah. I think
that's a better example.

00:04:59.231 --> 00:05:00.833
-Yes.
-So they buried, okay.

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:03.035
-So, then,
they had me get in the tank

00:05:03.035 --> 00:05:05.704
with our incredible
stunt coordinator, Katie Rowe.

00:05:05.704 --> 00:05:06.905
-So you're not in there alone.

00:05:06.905 --> 00:05:08.474
-I'm not in there alone.
-That's Katie there.

00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:10.243
-That's Katie there and she's
in full scuba gear

00:05:10.243 --> 00:05:12.645
with like a breathing tank.

00:05:12.645 --> 00:05:13.779
And then, you can see,
in that one,

00:05:13.779 --> 00:05:15.214
that's like how much air
I have to breathe,

00:05:15.214 --> 00:05:17.282
but that's when the light
is cracked.

00:05:17.282 --> 00:05:19.718
So, then, they shut the lid
and you're just in darkness

00:05:19.718 --> 00:05:21.653
and you're like here.
-Right.

00:05:21.653 --> 00:05:24.357
-And then, they bury you alive.

00:05:24.357 --> 00:05:25.391
They put dirt on it.
-Oof!

00:05:25.391 --> 00:05:26.625
-So you hear like,
"Ph-th, ph-th."

00:05:26.625 --> 00:05:28.861
And there's a little
walkie-talkie in there.

00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:30.829
And underneath me is a grate

00:05:30.829 --> 00:05:34.033
and underneath the grate
is many jets.

00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:35.468
Like -- [ Laughs ]

00:05:35.468 --> 00:05:36.802
-I mean, it's really impressive.

00:05:36.802 --> 00:05:39.171
-It's crazy.
-Because you seem like --

00:05:39.171 --> 00:05:41.107
Are you not someone
who gets claustrophobic?

00:05:41.107 --> 00:05:42.108
-I am.
-You are?!

00:05:42.108 --> 00:05:43.442
-Yeah. This was like
a nightmare.

00:05:43.442 --> 00:05:44.877
-Yeah.
-But it's also fun.

00:05:44.877 --> 00:05:47.913
Well, I couldn't help
but feel, the whole time,

00:05:47.913 --> 00:05:49.549
I was like, "Wow, look at me!

00:05:49.549 --> 00:05:51.350
-Yeah.
-I'm in a movie!"

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:53.419
Like it felt -- because there
was like a crane shot

00:05:53.419 --> 00:05:55.688
and then, on the walkie,
they'd be like, "3, 2,"

00:05:55.688 --> 00:05:57.890
and then the jets would go
and then they would blast me

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:00.326
out of the hole
and like it was 40°

00:06:00.326 --> 00:06:03.062
because the desert,
turns out, it's cold.

00:06:03.062 --> 00:06:04.530
-That's how they getcha.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:07.065
That's how the desert gets you.
-That's how the desert gets you.

00:06:07.065 --> 00:06:08.467
You like learned about it

00:06:08.467 --> 00:06:10.136
watching those
Bugs Bunny cartoons.

00:06:10.136 --> 00:06:11.370
It was never cold.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.005
-Never cold.
You think a bunch of roadrunners

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:14.173
are going to be your friends.

00:06:14.173 --> 00:06:15.808
You get there
and you freeze your ass off.

00:06:15.808 --> 00:06:18.310
Yeah, it's terrible.
And they would blast me out

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:20.279
and then we had
to do it like 20 times.

00:06:20.279 --> 00:06:22.181
I do look very happy
in that photo, I think,

00:06:22.181 --> 00:06:23.282
because I'm like, "Look at me!

00:06:23.282 --> 00:06:25.518
Doing stunts."
-You were like,

00:06:25.518 --> 00:06:26.619
-But it was terrifying.
-"Let's do this one,

00:06:26.619 --> 00:06:28.321
for if I'm ever
on a talk show about it."

00:06:28.321 --> 00:06:31.890
-"Oh, I'll file this away
for the talk show."

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:35.094
No, but then, because you're
in filthy water --

00:06:35.094 --> 00:06:36.762
You can see it
in the other picture.

00:06:36.762 --> 00:06:38.197
-Yeah, we do have a good --
I think this is

00:06:38.197 --> 00:06:39.865
the filthy water shot.
-You can see like

00:06:39.865 --> 00:06:42.468
there's like dead bugs
and there's like gross stuff.

00:06:42.468 --> 00:06:46.705
And then, I got a terrible rash
from the filthy water.

00:06:46.705 --> 00:06:48.641
-Well, what a wonderful --

00:06:48.641 --> 00:06:50.342
-And the scene is in the show

00:06:50.342 --> 00:06:52.378
for, mm, seven seconds?
-Is it really?

00:06:52.378 --> 00:06:55.014
-I think so.
But it's beautiful!

00:06:55.014 --> 00:06:57.016
And it felt really cool.

00:06:57.016 --> 00:06:58.751
-This is a show about --

00:06:58.751 --> 00:07:02.221
It's about people connecting
via chips in their head,

00:07:02.221 --> 00:07:04.756
-Yeah.
-to be more connected to,

00:07:04.756 --> 00:07:06.058
I guess, their partners.
-Well, to their partner.

00:07:06.058 --> 00:07:07.493
-Both: Yeah.

00:07:07.493 --> 00:07:09.729
-My character is married
to this like sort of

00:07:09.729 --> 00:07:14.367
Elon Musk-like tech wiz recluse.

00:07:14.367 --> 00:07:15.534
I don't know if Elon Musk
is a recluse.

00:07:15.534 --> 00:07:17.002
He doesn't seem like it, but.

00:07:17.002 --> 00:07:18.537
-I think he's pretty out there.

00:07:18.537 --> 00:07:20.739
-Actually, he does seem like
he goes out a lot.

00:07:20.739 --> 00:07:21.774
-I mean, not right now.

00:07:21.774 --> 00:07:23.075
Everybody's a little bit
of a recluse.

00:07:23.075 --> 00:07:24.410
But there's the wonderful
Ray Romano...

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:26.612
-Ugh!
-...who is just the best.

00:07:26.612 --> 00:07:28.147
-The best.
-He plays your father

00:07:28.147 --> 00:07:30.182
and his wife on the show
really steals it

00:07:30.182 --> 00:07:33.619
because she is played by,

00:07:33.619 --> 00:07:35.587
I guess --
what would you call it?

00:07:35.587 --> 00:07:37.457
-Synthetic partner.
-Synthetic partner.

00:07:37.457 --> 00:07:39.792
That's great.
-[ Laughs ] Your eyes.

00:07:39.792 --> 00:07:41.760
You're like, "Okay."
-That wasn't what I was
going to say.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:43.662
-Yeah, sure.
You were going to say sex doll.

00:07:43.662 --> 00:07:45.431
-Sex doll,
but this is really fun.

00:07:45.431 --> 00:07:47.600
The author of the book
that it's based on --

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:50.236
-Yeah, Alissa Nutting,
who's a genius.

00:07:50.236 --> 00:07:52.437
-She cast the synthetic partner.

00:07:52.437 --> 00:07:55.674
-Well, that's her face,
because you got to pay

00:07:55.674 --> 00:07:57.242
for the rights to someone's
face, if you're going

00:07:57.242 --> 00:07:58.577
to be putting them
on a synthetic partner

00:07:58.577 --> 00:07:59.644
on TV.
-Is that a true story?

00:07:59.644 --> 00:08:00.679
-That's a true story.

00:08:00.679 --> 00:08:02.648
So they were like, "Well,

00:08:02.648 --> 00:08:05.518
we can hire someone and pay them
for the rights to their face."

00:08:05.518 --> 00:08:07.286
She was like,
"Or I could do it for free."

00:08:07.286 --> 00:08:09.254
-Wow.
-And so, that's Hollywood.

00:08:09.254 --> 00:08:11.824
-Then, we can save money
for our tank stunt.

00:08:11.824 --> 00:08:16.862
-To blast Cristin out
in a filthy tank of water.

00:08:16.862 --> 00:08:18.997
-Hey, thank you for --

00:08:18.997 --> 00:08:20.733
Filthy Tank of Water
was originally

00:08:20.733 --> 00:08:22.334
what they were going to call
the Staten Island Ferry.

00:08:22.334 --> 00:08:23.936
-I know. That's right.
[ Laughter ]

